<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Helian Bobo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">381–425</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Liu Bobo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Helian Bobo was the founder of the Xia state, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and was of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiefu">Tiefu</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> origin. Born Liu Bobo, he broke away from Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> in 407 and proclaimed himself Heavenly King of Great Xia. He built the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongwan">Tongwan</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> in 418 and ruled until his death in 425.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Liu Bobo was a younger son of Liu Weichen, chief of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiefu">Tiefu</a> tribe, and a descendant of the southern chanyu Liu Qubei. In 391 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-daowu-of-northern-wei">Tuoba Gui</a> of Northern Wei captured his father's capital Yueba, Liu Weichen was killed by his own men, and his clan was slaughtered. Bobo escaped to Moyigan of the Poduoluo tribe, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of Later Qin, who married him to one of his daughters.</p><p>Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yao-xing">Yao Xing</a> of Later Qin was struck by the tall, eloquent young man and, against his brother Yao Yong's warning that Bobo was cruel and treacherous, made him a general and the Duke of Wuyuan, with some thirty thousand tribesmen to defend Shuofang. When Yao Xing made peace with Northern Wei in 407, Bobo rebelled, killed his father-in-law Moyigan and claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yu-the-great">Yu the Great</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>For years he refused to settle in a city, relying on the mobility of his cavalry to raid Later Qin where it was weak. In 407 he ambushed Tufa Rutan of Southern Liang in a canyon, and in 409 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yao-xing">Yao Xing</a> himself barely escaped him at Ercheng. In 413 he had his general Chigan Ali build <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongwan">Tongwan</a>; workmen were executed if an iron wedge could pierce the steamed earth of its walls. That year he took the surname Helian, which Chen Sanping derives from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> word for sky.</p><p>When Liu Yu of Jin destroyed Later Qin in 417 and then left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changan">Chang'an</a> to his young son Liu Yizhen, Bobo's sons isolated the city and crushed its retreating garrison, and Bobo stacked the skulls of the Jin dead into a mound. He then took the title of emperor, but kept his capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongwan">Tongwan</a>. Traditional historians describe him as growing ever more cruel, killing on a whim and mutilating officials who looked at him or contradicted him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 424 his plan to replace Crown Prince Helian Gui with Helian Lun set his sons fighting; Gui killed Lun and was killed in turn by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helian-chang">Helian Chang</a>, whom Bobo then made crown prince. Bobo died in the Yong'an Hall at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongwan">Tongwan</a> in late 425, aged forty-five. Helian Chang had twenty-five thousand labourers build the Jiaping Mausoleum, burned the halls built above it and offered several thousand horses as a sacrifice.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Helian Bobo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Liu Bobo</b>, of the Tiefu Xiongnu, was a commander under Later Qin who broke away in 407 and proclaimed himself Heavenly King of Great Xia, claiming descent from the ancient Xia dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/helian-xia">Helian Xia</a></li></ul></section>
